CM Y junction whose second arm runs one segment and then bends: a degree-3
CM junction the section-graph Hallen solve refuses (FND-162), because a
CM one-segment section has one collocation row for its two constants. It falls
CM back to the per-wire basis and solves to a negative resistance (Re Z = -5.127
CM ohm at 14.2 MHz) - a fixture for the negative-resistance caveat and the
CM infinite-VSWR worker path, which need a deck the solver still gets wrong. The
CM one-segment run sits between the node and a bend: until FND-227 it was a
CM one-segment arm with a free tip (Re Z = -5.441), which the free-end refinement
CM now splits into thirds and the section graph solves. Before that, a stem-fed Y
CM with three 11-segment arms, and an inverted-V (FND-167).
CE
GW 1 11 0 0 0 0 0 3 .001
GW 2 1 0 0 3 -1 0 4 .001
GW 4 5 -1 0 4 -1 0 5 .001
GW 3 11 0 0 3 2 0 5 .001
GE 0
EX 0 1 1 0 1.0 0.0
FR 0 1 0 0 14.2 0
EN
